ight in the  center of the banking district in London  but I looked at Psalm 1 over this week  and thought well when the person who  wrote this Psalm wrote it did they mean  it to be a protest song what did they  mean it to be something else well let's  have a look and see happy and blessed  first the psalmist is the one who  rejects the way of the wicked and  instead follows the way of God that  person will be just like a tree planted  by the water site Salman is not only a  psalm in its own right but it kind of  introduces the whole of the Book of  Psalms and it contains many of the  characteristics of Hebrew poetry that  reappear over and over again throughout  the book and Hebrew poetry is really not  much like english language poetry it's  quite functions quite differently in the  English language if you know your poetry  you'll be well aware that poetry is  built by playing with rhythm and rhyme  and meter and sometimes just the found  of the world I don't know whether you  know the poetry of Gerard Manley Hopkins  but there is a poet for whom if you  read his poetry silently in your head  whilst you're looking at the page you  keep noticing that some of the lines  don't make sense so have you made this  word up and how does the grammar work  there because actually I don't know what  he means but read it out loud and catch  the rhythm and the sound of the words  and suddenly you see what it is you  means because it makes sense out loud TS  Eliot was a bit the same now TS Eliot's  poem poetry does make sense as you read  it on the page but it makes better sense  when you read this out loud because then  you see that building of rhythm and the  repetition of those little tropes over  and over again while Hebrew poetry  doesn't work quite like that it works by  ordering ideas and an idea in a Hebrew  poem will be repeated or developed or it  will be set in contrast with something  quite different in order to put the  spotlight on the real point and that's  what h